<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Constructing antiderivatives (Section 5.1)</title>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Using what you know from your first semester of calculus,
        consider the function below and sketch its derivative to
        the right.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="0% 0%">
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              The graph of a degree four polynomial
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="02-graph-1-a">
              <xi:include href="prefigure/02-graph-1.xml"/>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              An empty coordinate grid
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="02-empty-1">
              <xi:include href="prefigure/02-empty-1.xml"/>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="0% 0%">
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              The graph of a degree four polynomial
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="02-graph-1-b">
              <xi:include href="prefigure/02-graph-1.xml"/>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              The graph of the derivative is a cubic polynomial
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="02-solution-1">
              <xi:include href="prefigure/02-solution-1.xml"/>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="0% 0%">
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              The graph of a cubic polynomial
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="02-graph-2-a">
              <xi:include href="prefigure/02-graph-2.xml"/>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              An empty coordinate grid
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="02-empty-2">
              <xi:include href="prefigure/02-empty-1.xml"/>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="0% 0%">
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              The graph of a cubic polynomial
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="02-graph-2-b">
              <xi:include href="prefigure/02-graph-2.xml"/>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              The graph of the deriviative of the cubic polynomial
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="02-solution-2">
              <xi:include href="prefigure/02-solution-2.xml"/>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Now let's try to go the other way.  Consider the function
        shown below and sketch an <em>antiderivative</em>---that
        is, a function whose derivative is shown---to the right.
        Perhaps you could begin by thinking about where an
        antiderivative is increasing and where it is decreasing.
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="0% 0%">
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            The graph of a linear function having negative slope
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="02-graph-3-a">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/02-graph-3.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            An empty coordinate grid
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="02-empty-3">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/02-empty-1.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </statement>
    <solution component="aaa-solutions">
      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="0% 0%">
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            The graph of a linear function having negative slope
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="02-graph-3-b">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/02-graph-3.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            The graph of an antiderivative of a linear function having
            negative slope
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="02-solution-3">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/02-solution-3.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Now we're going to see how to do a better job of the
        last task.  Shown below on the left is the graph of a
        function <m>f(x)</m> that is the derivative of an
        unknown function <m>F(x)</m>.  We say that <m>F(x)</m>
        is an <em>antiderivative</em> of <m>f(x)</m> because
        <m>F'(x) = f(x)</m>.  We would like to deduce
        information about <m>F(x)</m>.
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="0% 0%">
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            The graph of a piecewise linear function
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="02-graph-4-a">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/02-graph-4.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            An empty coordinate grid
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="02-empty-4">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/02-empty-2.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Remember that you are looking at the graph of
          <m>F'(x) = f(x)</m>.  On what interval(s) is <m>F</m>
          increasing and on what intervals is it decreasing?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          The function <m>F</m> increases when <m>F'=f</m> is
          positive, which means on the intervals <m>0\lt x \lt 2</m>
          and <m>5\lt x \lt 7</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the graph of <m>f'(x)</m> on the empty axes on
          the right above.  Notice that this is the same as
          <m>F''(x)</m>:  since <m>F'(x) = f(x)</m>, then
          <m>F''(x) = f'(x)</m>.  On what interval(s) is the
          graph of <m>F</m> concave up?  concave down? neither?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <image width="50%">
          <shortdescription>
            The graph of the derivative of a function that is
            piecewise linear
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="02-graph-4-deriv">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/02-graph-4-deriv.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
        <p>
          The graph of <m>F</m> is concave up with <m>F'' = f'</m> is
          positive, which is on the intervals <m>0\lt x \lt 1</m> and
          <m>4\lt x \lt 6</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          At what points does <m>F</m> have a relative minimum?
          maximum?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          <m>F</m> has a relative minimum when the derivative changes
          from negative to positive which happens at <m>x=5</m>.
          There is a relative maximum when the derivative changes from
          positive to negative which happens at <m>x=2</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate the integral <m>\int_0^1 f(x)~dx</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          Look at the area under the graph to find
          <m>\int_0^1f(x)~dx = 1/2\cdot1\cdot1 = 1/2</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus tells us that
          <md>
            \int_0^1 f(x)~dx = \int_0^1 F'(x)~dx = F(1) - F(0).
          </md>
          Suppose we are told that <m>F(0) = -1</m>.  What is
          <m>F(1)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          <m>F(1)=F(0)+\int_0^1f(x)~dx = -1 + 1/2 = -1/2</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the same way, find the values of <m>F(x)</m> in the
          table below.
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>x \vphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>7</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>F(x) \vphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>x \vphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>7</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>F(x) \vphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-1/2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-1/2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-3/2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-3/2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using all the information you have found so far, sketch
          the graph of <m>F(x)</m> below.
        </p>
        <image width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
            An empty coordinate grid
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="02-empty-anti">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/02-empty-anti.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <image width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
            The graph of an antiderivative of the piecewise linear
            function
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                     label="02-solution-anti">
            <xi:include href="prefigure/02-solution-anti.xml"/>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose there is another antiderivative <m>G</m> where
          <m>G'(x) = f(x)</m> and <m>G(0) = 0</m>.  Complete
          the table of values of <m>G</m>.
        </p>
        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>x \vphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>7</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>G(x) \vphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\hphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <tabular>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
          <col halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>x \vphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell>0</cell>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>3</cell>
            <cell>4</cell>
            <cell>5</cell>
            <cell>6</cell>
            <cell>7</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>G(x) \vphantom{\Huge M}</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1/2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>1/2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-1/2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-1/2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>0</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How are the graphs of <m>F</m> and <m>G</m> related to
          one another?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          The graph of <m>G</m> is the same as the graph of <m>F</m>
          shifted up by one unit.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        In the same way, consider the graph of the function
        <m>f'(x)</m> shown below.
      </p>
      <image width="40%">
        <shortdescription>
          The graph of another piecewise linear
          function
        </shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                   label="02-ftc-graph">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/02-ftc-graph.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>f(0) = 4</m>, what is the value <m>f(6)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          <md>
            <mrow>
              f(6) = \amp f(0) + \int_0^6f'(x)~dx
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              = \amp 4 + 0 = 4
            </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the value of <m>f</m> at each of its critical
          points.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          <m>f(3/2) = 4 + 3/4 = 4.75</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>f(4) = f(3/2) -15/4 = 1</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is the global maximum and what is the global
          minimum of <m>f</m> on the interval <m>[0,6]</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="aaa-solutions">
        <p>
          The global maximum and minimum occur at either a critical
          point or one of the endpoints:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>f(0)=4</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>f(3/2)=4.75</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>f(4)=1</m></p></li>
            <li><p><m>f(6)=4</m></p></li>
          </ul>
          so the global maximum if <m>f(3/2)=4.75</m> and the global
          minimum is <m>f(4)=1</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
